{#-
  state_color(entity_id, state=none): the colour HA's frontend (and the theme) gives this state,
  i.e. --state-<domain>-<state>-color, falling back to the domain's and then the global
  active colour. Returned as "from var(...) r g b" because Mushroom wraps icon_color
  in rgb(); relative colour syntax turns the CSS colour back into r g b channels.
  Off-like states all get the one idle grey (--app-icon-shape-off-rgb) the room tiles and Mushroom
  cards use. Keep the off list in step with off_states in app_room_card_device.
  Usage: {% from 'state_color.jinja' import state_color %}{{ state_color('lock.front_door') }}
  A bare domain borrows that domain's colours: state_color('light', states('switch.x')).

  state_css_color(entity_id, state=none): the same colour as a complete CSS colour, rgb(...).
  For Mushroom 5's template card (color, badge_color, and icon_color, which it renames to color):
  it passes the value to CSS as is, so the bare "from var(...) r g b" from state_color is invalid there.
-#}
{%- macro state_color(entity_id, state=none) -%}
{%- set domain = entity_id.split('.')[0] -%}
{%- set s = state if state is not none else states(entity_id) -%}
{%- if s in ['off', 'unavailable', 'unknown', 'idle', 'standby', 'closed', 'not_home', 'docked', 'paused'] -%}
var(--app-icon-shape-off-rgb)
{%- else -%}
from var(--state-{{ domain }}-{{ s }}-color, var(--state-{{ domain }}-active-color, var(--state-active-color))) r g b
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro state_css_color(entity_id, state=none) -%}
rgb({{ state_color(entity_id, state) }})
{%- endmacro -%}
